I have a complete picture. Here is the analysis:

```json
[
  {
    "file": "Record-App/Sources/Business/UseCases/PipelineCoordinator.swift",
    "line": 104,
    "summary": "Zombie cleanup task: early return when archivePassEnabled==false skips cleanupTask?.cancel()",
    "failure_scenario": "Call startCleanupPass once with archivePassEnabled==true — a Task is now running in cleanupTask. Feature flag is flipped to false at runtime (or a new coordinator is constructed with false). Caller calls startCleanupPass again. The guard fires and returns immediately, skipping the cleanupTask?.cancel() and activeRecordingId assignment on lines 106-107. The previous Task continues executing performCleanupTranscription against the old recordingId, calling saveDraftTranscript and updateTranscript for a recording that is no longer the active context. isActiveRecording() on line 145 will wrongly return true because activeRecordingId was never updated, so the stale result is accepted and onCleanupStateChanged(.complete) fires for the wrong recording."
  },
  {
    "file": "Record-App/Sources/Business/UseCases/PipelineCoordinator.swift",
    "line": 107,
    "summary": "activeRecordingId update skipped when disabled — isActiveRecording guard becomes stale",
    "failure_scenario": "When archivePassEnabled is false, activeRecordingId is never updated to the new recordingId. If a prior cleanup task is running (zombie from finding #1) and it calls isActiveRecording(oldRecordingId), it returns true because activeRecordingId still holds oldRecordingId. This means the staleness guard that is supposed to discard results for superseded recordings does not fire, and the zombie task's result passes the guard and is delivered to the UI as a valid cleanup result."
  },
  {
    "file": "Record-App/Sources/Business/UseCases/CleanupTranscriptionUseCase.swift",
    "line": 50,
    "summary": "NullTranscriptReader inverts nil-transcriptReader semantics — nil was likely intended to mean 'skip draft-save', but wires up always-save instead",
    "failure_scenario": "A caller who wants to opt out of the draft-save side effect might reasonably pass transcriptReader: nil, expecting the feature to be disabled. Instead, NullTranscriptReader is installed, which always returns nil from getTranscriptDraft. The nil check on line 154 then always triggers, and saveDraftTranscript is always called. The only way to suppress draft-saving is to inject a reader that returns non-nil — the exact opposite of the intuitive contract. This is an inversion-of-control bug that will surprise any caller who passes nil expecting no-op behaviour."
  },
  {
    "file": "Record-App/Sources/Business/UseCases/CleanupTranscriptionUseCase.swift",
    "line": 155,
    "summary": "Empty-string draft corrupts the idempotency guard for untranscribed recordings",
    "failure_scenario": "If recording.transcript is nil (recording was captured but never transcribed — e.g. live transcription failed or was never started), the fallback `recording.transcript?.fullText ?? \"\"` saves \"\" as the draft. On the next cleanup pass for the same recording, getTranscriptDraft returns \"\" (non-nil), so the idempotency guard treats the draft as already saved and skips it. The empty string is now permanently locked in as the draft. Any downstream feature that restores the original live transcript from the draft (e.g. an undo of the archive pass) will restore nothing instead of surfacing an error or skipping restoration."
  },
  {
    "file": "Record-App/Sources/Business/UseCases/CleanupTranscriptionUseCase.swift",
    "line": 154,
    "summary": "saveDraftTranscript fires before updateTranscript in error path — draft written, transcript not updated, no rollback",
    "failure_scenario": "saveDraftTranscript on line 156 succeeds and persists the draft. Then updateTranscript on line 161 throws (network drop, DuckDB lock, etc.). The function re-throws, leaving the database in a state where draft_transcript is set but transcript_text is still the old live value. On the next cleanup pass, the idempotency guard sees a non-nil draft and skips the draft-save — correctly. But it still calls updateTranscript, which may fail again. If the failure is permanent (e.g. the recording row was deleted), the orphaned draft row can never be cleared because the use case has no compensating delete path. This is a partial-write invariant that was not present before the draft-save block was added."
  },
  {
    "file": "Record-App/Sources/Business/UseCases/PipelineCoordinator.swift",
    "line": 67,
    "summary": "recoverPendingSession bypasses archivePassEnabled guard entirely",
    "failure_scenario": "recoverPendingSession calls performCleanupTranscription directly, without checking archivePassEnabled. If a user disables the archive/cleanup pass via settings, app relaunch still triggers recoverPendingSession for any pending session, which runs a full cleanup transcription pass against the user's explicit preference. The guard added in startCleanupPass is the only enforcement point and is bypassed by this recovery path."
  }
]
```

